Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: HÄRDLE, Wolfgang
Author-Name: TURLACH, Berwin
Title: Nonparametric approaches to generalized linear models
Abstract: In this paper we investigate the gains of using nonparametric estimation methods in a family of models related to Generalised Linear Models. We focus especially on
discrete choice models. We give an overview on different nonparametric and semiparametric approaches in this setting. In particular we discuss estimation methods
such as average derivative estimation (ADE), semiparametric weighted least squares (Single Index Models, SIM), Projection Pursuit Regression (PPR) and Generalized
Additive Models (GAM). Their performance in practice and theory is compared.
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1992037
Creation-Date: 1992-07-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1992.html
File-Format: text/html
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:1992037

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: BAUWENS, Luc
Author-Email: luc.bauwens@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: CORE, Université catholique de Louvain, B-1348 Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium
Author-Name: RASQUERO, A.
Author-Workplace-Name: GREQE, Ecole des Hautes Etudes en Sciences Sociales, 2, rue de la Charité, Marseille, France
Title: Approximate HPD regions for testing residual autocorrelation using augmented regressions
Abstract: We evaluate two tests of residual autocorrelation in the linear regression model in a Bayesian framework. Each test checks if an approximate highest posterior density
region of the parameters of the autoregressive process of the error contains the null hypothesis. The approximation consists in computing the posterior density
of the coefficients of the AR process using augmented regressions. The first test uses the initial regression augmented with its lagged Bayesian residuals and can be
done with tables of the Fisher distribution. The second test augments the initial regression with lagged dependent and explanatory variables, and requires numerical
integration. The tests are evaluated through a small Monte-Carlo experiment, which indicates that the first test (easier to compute) is more powerful than the
second one.
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1992038
Creation-Date: 1992-08-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1992.html
