Author-Name: RAY, Indrajit
Author-Workplace-Name: Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE), Université catholique de Louvain (UCL), Louvain la Neuve, Belgium
Title: A Remark on the Consistency Principle for Games in Strategic Form
Abstract: If a solution (on a closed [exp.2] family of games) satisfies dummy axiom (DUM) and independence of irrelevant strategies (lIS) then it also satisfies consistency (CONS) (proposition 2.21, Peleg and Tijs, 1996). The converse of this proposition is not true: CONS implies neither DUM nor lIS as shown in two simple examples, using coalition- proof Nash equilibrium. However, CONS implies weak dummy axiom (WDUM) which can replace DUM in the above proposition.
Classification-JEL: C72
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1996017
Creation-Date: 1996-05-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1996.html
File-Format: text/html
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:1996017

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: SMITH, Tony E.
Author-Workplace-Name: Department of Systems, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia
Author-Name: ZENOU, Yves
Author-Workplace-Name: CORE, Universite catholique de Louvain and ERMES, Universite de Paris II
Title: Dual Labor Markets, Urban Unemployment and Multicentric Cities
Abstract: A two-sector model of urban unemployment is developed which focuses on the formation of a secondary sector under conditions in which a demand shock in the primary sector leads to a sharp increase in unemployment. The optimal location in the secondary sector (treated as a single firm) is shown to be at the edge of the city, giving rise to a multicentric urban spatial structure. Within this spatial setting, the main result of the paper is to establish conditions under which the new labor market equilibrium involves not only a decrease in unemployment, but also an increase in net income for those unemployed. These results are extended to the case in which all unemployment benefits are financed by local taxation of firms. In this context, it is shown that when taxation discourages entry of a secondary sector, there may be profit incentives for the primary sector to subsidize the entry of a secondary sector.
Classification-JEL: J41, R14
Keywords: Efficiency wage, minimum wage, edge city, local taxation
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1996018
Creation-Date: 1996-05-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1996.html
