Author-Email: claude.daspremont@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE), Université catholique de Louvain (UCL), Louvain la Neuve, Belgium
Author-Name: DOS SANTOS FERREIRA, Rodolphe
Author-Workplace-Name: BETA, Université Louis Pasteur, Strasbourg, France
Author-Name: GERARD-VARET, Louis-André
Author-Workplace-Name: GREQAM, Ecole des Hautes Etudes en Sciences Sociales, Marseille, France
Title: Contestability and the indeterminacy of free entry equilibria
Abstract: A general notion of market perfect contestability is introduced. It coincides with the definition given by Baumol et al. under Bertrand competition, but is compatible with other forms of competition : Cournot competition as well as monopolistic competition. Using this notion, we illustrate the fact that the number of active firms in free entry equilibrium may be largely indeterminate and different levels of positive profits may in many cases be sustained. This is shown to be true, in spite of market perfect contestability, either under Cournot competition or under product differentiation. Examples are given for both cases. Appropriate conditions of increasing returns are required.
Classification-JEL: D5, D43
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1997073
Creation-Date: 1997-10-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1997.html
File-Format: text/html
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:1997073

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: SHALEV, Jonathan
Author-Workplace-Name: Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE), Université catholique de Louvain (UCL), Louvain la Neuve, Belgium
Title: Loss aversion equilibrium revised
Abstract: The Nash equilibrium solution concept for games is based on the assumption of expected utility maximization. Reference dependent utility functions (in which utility is determined not only by an outcome, but also by the relationship of the outcome to a reference point) are a better predictor of behavior than expected utility. In particular, loss aversion is an important element of such utility functions. We extend games to include loss aversion characteristics of the players. We define two types of loss-aversion equilibrium, a solution concept endogenizing reference points. The two types reflect different procedures of updating reference points during the game. Reference points emerge as expressions of anticipation which are fulfilled. We show existence of myopic loss-aversion equilibrium for any extended game, and compare it to Nash equilibrium. Comparative statics show that an increase in loss aversion of one player can affect her and other players’ payoffs in different directions.
Classification-JEL: C72
Keywords: loss aversion, reference dependence, equilibrium
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1997074
Creation-Date: 1997-10-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1997.html
File-Format: text/html
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:1997074

