Author-Name: CALVET, Laurent
Author-Workplace-Name: Yale University, and CERAS, Paris
Author-Name: GRANDMONT, Jean-Michel
Author-Email: grandmon@pareto.ensae.fr
Author-Workplace-Name: CREST and CNRS, Paris
Author-Name: LEMAIRE, Isabelle
Author-Workplace-Name: CREST-INSEE, Paris
Title: Heterogeneous probabilities in complete  asset markets
Abstract: We show in this paper how, in a model of assets exchange in complete competitive markets, heterogeneity of the agent’s sub jective probabilities generates aggregate expenditures for Arrow-Debreu securities that have the gross substitutability property, with the consequences that competitive equilibrium is unique, stable in any tatonnement process, and that the weak axiom of revealed preferences is satisﬁed in the aggregate. For this result, heterogeneity is required to be highest among people who have the largest risk aversion
Classification-JEL: C62, D50, G10
Keywords: Heterogeneity, subjective probabilities, complete asset markets. 
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1998019
Creation-Date: 1998-03-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1998.html
File-Format: text/html
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:1998019

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: JOUVET, Pierre-André
Author-Email: jouvet@romarin.univ-aix.fr
Author-Workplace-Name: GREQAM, Marseille and GREMARS, Université de Lille III
Title: Voluntary contributions with uncertainty: the environmental quality.
Abstract: This paper presents an overlapping generations model of environmental externalities with a depollution technology. Each agent concerned by the environmental degradation can voluntarily contribute in order to reduce it. Contributing to the environmental quality means financing depollution activities and uncertainty concerns its efficiency since it's not always clear or certain what effects will result from a reduction of pollution. We show that if an agent is sufficiently risk averse, voluntary contribution is a decreasing function of average efficiency of depollution technology. If on the contrary, the substitution effect is weaker than the pollution effect, the opposite holds. We show that precaution about environmental quality has two possible consequencies which depend on agent risk aversion. Therefore, understanding the implication of precautionary attitude, leads us to the consideration of the agents' risk aversion characterization and studying the effect of an increase in risk aversion implies a knowledge on prudent attitude. So, we have to conclude that precaution and risk aversion are two complementary and indissociable concepts.
Classification-JEL: D62, D80, H41, Q29
Keywords: Environmental management, Uncertainty, Public goods, Voluntary contributions, Precaution, Risk
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1998020
Creation-Date: 1998-03-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1998.html
File-Format: text/html
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:1998020

