Author-Email: loparic@core.ucl.ac.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE), Université catholique de Louvain (UCL), Louvain la Neuve, Belgium
Author-Name: POCHET, Yves
Author-Workplace-Name: Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE), Université catholique de Louvain (UCL), Louvain la Neuve, Belgium
Author-Name: WOLSEY, Laurence 
Author-Email: laurence.wolsey@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE), Université catholique de Louvain (UCL), Louvain la Neuve, Belgium
Title: The uncapacitated lot-sizing problem with sales and safety stocks
Abstract: We examine a variant of the uncapacitated lot-sizing model of Wagner-Whitin involving sales instead of fixed demands, and lower bounds on stocks. Two extended formulations are presented, as well as a dynamic programming algorithm and a complete description of the convex hull of solutions. When the lower bounds on stocks are non-decreasing over time, it is possible to describe an extended formulation for the problem and a combinatorial separation algorithm for the convex hull of solutions. Finally when the lower bounds on stocks are constant, a simpler polyhedral description is obtained for the case of Wagner-Whitin costs.
Keywords: lot-sizing, production planning, mixed integer programming, integral polyhedra, extended formulations
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1999017
Creation-Date: 1999-03-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1999.html
File-Format: text/html
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:1999017

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: KOUTSOUGERAS, Leonidas
Author-Workplace-Name: School of Economic Studies, University of Manchester and Department of Econometrics, Tilburg University
Title: Market games with multiple trading posts
Abstract: We study market games with multiple posts per commodity. We provide some facts that characterize prices of commodities across posts and show the following results: (i) As the number of agents increases, the price variability across posts for a commodity becomes smaller and it becomes zero when the number of agents becomes inffinite, irrespectively of the distribution of characteristics in the economy. (ii) The set of equilibrium prices and allocations of a market game is a subset of the set of equilibria of another game with more trading posts per commodity. (iii) We demonstrate via an example that the inclusion can be strict, as there are equilibria with price disparities across posts for a commodity which cannot be captured with less trading posts. (iv) One can pass from an equilibrium of a market game into an equilibrium of a game with less trading posts per commodity, by consolidating posts where the price of a commodity is uniform.
Classification-JEL: C72
Keywords: trading posts, law of one price. 
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1999018
Creation-Date: 1999-03-22
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1999.html
File-Format: text/html
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:1999018

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: OTTAVIANO, Gianmarco
Author-Workplace-Name: Universita di Bologna, Italy, CORE, Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium and CEPR
Author-Name: THISSE, Jacques-François
Author-Email: jacques.thisse@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE), Université catholique de Louvain (UCL), 1348 Louvain la Neuve, Belgium
Title: Monopolistic competition, multiproduct firms and optimum product diversity
Abstract: This paper tackles the issue of optimum product diversity in an imperfectly competitive market with small or large firms. First, it develops a quadratic utility model of monopolistic competition with horizontal product differentiation which avoids some of the main pitfalls of the S-D-S approach. Second, it extends the model to the case of multiproduct firms showing how product diversity is affected with respect to monopolistic competition. In particular, it is shown that monopolistic competition with single-product firms is the limiting case of oligopolistic competition with multiproduct firms when either varieties gets more and more differentiated or when the entry cost goes further and further down.
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1999019
Creation-Date: 1999-03-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1999.html
File-Format: text/html
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:1999019

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: MAULEON, Ana
Author-Workplace-Name: Department of Economic Analysis, Basque Country University
Author-Name: VANNETELBOSCH, Vincent
Author-Email: vv@bl.ehu.es
Author-Workplace-Name: Institute of Public Economics, Basque Country University
Title: Coalitional negotiation
Abstract: We develop a two-stage negotiation model to study the impact of costly inspections on both the coalition formation outcome and the per-member payoffs. In the first stage, the players are forming coalitions and inside each coalition formed the members share the coalition benefits. We adopt the largest consistent set (LCS) to predict which coalition structures are possibly stable. We also introduce a refinement,the largest cautious consistent set (LCCS). In the second stage, the inspection game takes place inside each coalition. For games with positive spillovers, many coalition structures may belong to the LCS under costless inspection. The grand coalition, which is the efficient coalition structure, always belongs to the LCS and is the unique one to belong to the LCCS. Under costly inspection, the grand coalition does not always belong to the LCS. Nevertheless, there exists inspection cost parameters such that the LCS singles out the grand coalition.
Classification-JEL: C70, C71, C72, C78 
Keywords: coalition formation, inspections, positive spillovers, largest consistent set. 
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 1999020
Creation-Date: 1999-03-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp1999.html
File-Format: text/html
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:1999020

