Author-Name: LEROUX, Marie - Louise
Author-Email: marie-louise.leroux@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: CORE, Université catholique de Louvain, B-1348 Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium
Author-Name: PONTHIERE, Grégory
Author-Email: Gregory.ponthiere@ens.fr
Author-Workplace-Name: Ecole Normale Supérieure, Paris, and Paris School of Economics, F-75014 Paris, France
Title: Utilitarianism and unequal longevities : A remedy?
Abstract: This paper re-examines a counterintuitive corollary of utilitarianism under unequal longevities: the tendency to redistribute resources from short-lived towards long-lived agents, against any intuition of compensation. It is shown that this corollary prevails not only under time-additive lifetime welfare, but, also, in general, under non-additive lifetime welfare, so that this counterintuitive redistributive corollary is a robust argument against utilitarianism. This paper studies a remedy to that counterintuitive corollary. This consists in imputing, when solving the social planner's problem, the consumption equivalent of a long life to the consumption of long- lived agents. We identify the conditions under which such a modified utilitarian optimum involves a compensation of short-lived agents with respect to the laissez-faire. That remedy is also applied to an economy with risky longevity, where short-lived agents are penalized not only by the limited opportunities to spread resources over time (due to a shorter life), but, also, by lost savings (due to unanticipated death).
Classification-JEL: D63, I12, I18, J18
Keywords: utilitarianism, differential longevity, compensation, redistribution, consumption equivalent
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2010043
Creation-Date: 2010-07-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2010.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2010043

