Author-Workplace-Name: Emory University, USA
Author-Name: SONG, Huasheng
Author-Workplace-Name: CRPE and College of Economics, Zhejiang University, China; Université catholique de Louvain, CORE, B-1348 Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium
Author-Name: VANDENBUSSCHE, Hylke
Author-Email: hylke.vandenbussche@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, CORE and IRES, B-1348 Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium; KULeuven-LICOS & CEPR
Title: Innovation, antidumping and retaliation
Abstract: We study the effect of contingency trade policy in a multi-country oligopoly model with and without R&D opportunities. We show that firms benefit from unilateral protection but initiate antidumping (AD) only against the targets domiciled in substantially smaller countries. Also, AD filings are more likely when firms face R&D opportunities. These results are consistent with recent empirical findings, namely, (1) actions are mostly between industrial and developing countries, (2) developing countries use AD to retaliate against industrial countries, and (3) AD is concentrated in R&D-intensive industries. Interestingly, intellectual property rights violations in developing countries have no connection to AD filings.
Classification-JEL: F12, F13, L13
Keywords: R&D, antidumping, intellectual property rights, reciprocal dumping
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2010064
Creation-Date: 2010-10-01
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2010.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2010064

