Author-Name: MAYNERIS, Florian
Author-Email: florian.mayneris@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, CORE & IRES, B-1348 Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium 
Author-Name: PONCET, Sandra
Author-Email: sandra.poncet@univ-paris1.fr
Author-Workplace-Name: Paris School of Economics, Université Paris 1 and CEPII
Title: Entry on difficult export markets by Chinese domestic firms: the role of foreign export spillovers
Abstract: In this study, we explore how the intensity of foreign export spillovers in China varies depending on the difficulty of entry on export markets. We rely on different proxies to define what a "difficult" country is and we find that the presence of surrounding foreign exporting firms helps domestic ones to start exporting, especially when destination countries are difficult. While on average exposure to foreign exporters is associated with a 10% increase of the probability that domestic firms from the same province start exporting the year after, the figure is around 50% higher when the targeted destination country is identified as difficult. Our results are consistent with the idea that exposure to foreign exporters helps to reduce the fixed cost of creating new trade linkages. Our finding hence suggests that the increasing presence of foreign exporting firms in China might contribute to the diversification of Chinese domestic firms' exports towards more difficult and previously inaccessible destinations.
Classification-JEL: F1, R12, L25
Keywords: foreign export spillovers, difficult markets, China
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2011063
Creation-Date: 2011-12-22
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2011.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2011063

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: MAYNERIS, Florian
Author-Email: florian.mayneris@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, CORE & IRES, B-1348 Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium 
Author-Name: PONCET, Sandra
Author-Email: sandra.poncet@univ-paris1.fr
Author-Workplace-Name: Paris School of Economics, Université Paris 1 and CEPII
Title: French firms at the conquest of Asian markets: the role of export spillovers
Abstract: In this study, we explore the role of export spillovers on the capacity of French firms to conquer Asian markets. We confirm, in the context of France, previous results emphasizing the positive impact of surrounding exporters on the probability that a firm starts exporting a given product to a given country. We find that export spillovers are more important for export starts to Asia than for export starts to other countries. Moreover, for the specific Asian destinations, we find evidence of a heterogeneous effect of export spillovers. The presence of surrounding exporting firms appears especially beneficial to small and less productive firms, and more intense for export starts to Asian countries characterized by low GDP per capita and tough administrative procedures on imports. Hence, export spillovers may help small firms to enter on the most difficult Asian markets.
Classification-JEL: F1, R12, L25
Keywords: export spillovers, Asia, new trade linkages
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2011064
Creation-Date: 2011-12-22
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2011.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2011064

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: GABSZEWICZ, Jean
Author-Email: jean.gabszewicz@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, CORE, B-1348 Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium 
Author-Name: TAROLA, Ornella
Author-Email: ornella.tarola@fastwebnet.it
Author-Workplace-Name: University of Rome “La Sapienza”, DTE, Italy
Title: Migration, wage differentials and fiscal competition
Abstract: We analyze the effects, on nonredistributive taxation and on migrations, of wage differentials existing between two countries (regions) differing by the size of their population. Residents, otherwise identical, are heterogeneous because they incur different migration costs. Each resident compares the post-tax amount of money at home with the one obtained abroad, including the cost of migration. The government in each country maximizes the tax product in order to provide the largest possible amount of public good. We first assume that the income of citizens are identical across countries. Then, we assume that wages differ from one country to the other. We prove the existence of an equilibrium for any configuration of wage and any different relative size of the countries (regions). Then, we compute and characterize the equilibrium for any set of parameters, size and wage differential.
Keywords: migration, taxation, wage differentials
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2011065
Creation-Date: 2011-12-22
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2011.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2011065

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: BOADWAY, Robin
Author-Email: boadwayr@econ.queensu.ca
Author-Workplace-Name: Queen’s University, Canada & CESifo
Author-Name: PESTIEAU, Pierre
Author-Email: p.pestieau@ulg.ac.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, CORE, B-1348 Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium and Université de Liège, B-4000 Liège, Belgium
Title: Indirect taxes for redistribution: should necessity goods be favored?
Abstract: The Atkinson-Stiglitz Theorem shows that with weakly separable preferences, differential commod- ity taxes are not needed if an optimal nonlinear income tax is imposed. Redistributive objectives can be achieved with the income tax alone even if goods differ considerably in their income elasticities of demand. Deaton showed that if the government is restricted to a linear progressive income tax along with commodity taxes, the latter are superfluous if preferences are not only weakly separable but also yield linear Engel curves whose slopes are common to all households. These have potentially strong policy implications since they suggest that the common practice of giving preferential commodity tax treatment to necessities is not warranted. Using the linear progressive income tax as an example and assuming the Deaton conditions are satisfied, we derive two contradictory results. First, if the income tax is less progressive than optimal, necessities should be taxed preferentially relative to luxuries. Second, if low-income households are income-constrained so are unable to afford any luxury goods, it may be optimal to tax necessity goods at higher rates than luxuries, depending on whether labor supply varies along the intensive or extensive margin. The analysis is extended to allow for nonlinear taxes and endogenous demand for a public good.
Classification-JEL: H21, H23
Keywords: optimal income tax, Atkinson-Stiglitz Theorem, indirect taxes
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2011066
Creation-Date: 2011-12-31
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2011.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2011066

