Author-Name: PESTIEAU, Pierre
Author-Email: p.pestieau@ulg.ac.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université de Liège; Université catholique de Louvain, CORE, B-1348 Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium; Paris School of Economics & CEPR
Author-Name: PONTHIERE, Grégory
Author-Email: gregory.ponthiere@ens.fr
Author-Workplace-Name: Ecole Normale Supérieure, Paris & Paris School of Economics
Title: The public economics of increasing longevity
Abstract: One of the greatest success stories in our societies is that people are living longer, life expectancy at birth being now above 80 years. Whereas the lengthening of life opens huge opportunities for individuals if extra years are spent in prosperity and good health, it is however often regarded as a source of problems for policy-makers. The goal of this paper is to examine the key policy challenges raised by increasing longevity. For that purpose, we first pay attention to the representation of individual preferences, and to the normative foundations of the economy, and, then, we consider the challenges raised for the design of the social security system, pension policies, preventive health policies, the provision of long term care, as well as for long-run economic growth.
Classification-JEL: H21, H55, I12, I13, J10
Keywords: life expectancy, mortality, public policy
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2012005
Creation-Date: 2012-02-21
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2012.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2012005

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: BRECHET, Thierry
Author-Email: thierry.brechet@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, CORE and Louvain School of Economics, B-1348 Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium
Author-Name: MEUNIER, Guy
Author-Email: guy.meunier@ivry.inra.fr
Author-Workplace-Name: INRA-UR, ALISS & Ecole Polytecnique, France
Title: Are clean technology and environmental quality conflicting policy goals?
Abstract: In this paper we analyze the effects of an environmental policy on the diffusion of a clean technology in an economy where firms compete on the output market. We show that the share of adopting firms is non-monotonic with the stringency of the environmental policy, and that the adoption of the clean technology may well increase the pollution level. We also compare the effects of an emission tax and tradable pollution permits on welfare, technology adoption, and pollution level. We show that, depending on the stringency of the policy, either the tax or the permits can yield a higher degree of technology adoption and pollution. Actually, technology adoption and environmental quality may be conflicting in discriminating among the instruments.
Classification-JEL: H23, Q55, Q58
Keywords: innovation, technology adoption, environmental regulation
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2012006
Creation-Date: 2012-03-08
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2012.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2012006

