Author-Name: CARPANTIER, Jean-François
Author-Email: jean-francois.carpantier@uni.lu
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, IRES, Belgium; CREA, University of Luxembourg
Author-Name: DUFAYS, Arnaud
Author-Email: arnaud.dufays@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, CORE, Belgium
Title: Commodities volatility and the theory of storage
Abstract: One implication of the theory of storage states that commodity price volatility should increase when inventories are low. We document this volatility feature by estimating asymmetric volatility models for 16 commodity return series, on the period 1994-2011 and show how to account for this feature in Value-at-Risk forecasting. Our contribution is threefold: (i) This study is the first to investigate systematically the volatility implication of the theory of storage for a large panel of commodity types (agriculturals, metals, precious metals and tree crops); (ii) Since inventories are hard to measure and define, especially for high frequency data, we use in the volatility model positive return shocks as a new original proxy for inventories; (iii) We finally develop an original asymmetric version of the spline GARCH model and find that the inventory effect remains robust if we allow the unconditional variance to vary over time.
Keywords: asymmetries, commodities, inventory spline GARCH, VaR
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2012037
Creation-Date: 2012-10-11
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2012.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2012037

