Author-Name: RUSSO, Federica
Author-Workplace-Name: Center Leo Apostel, Vrije Universiteit Brussel
Title: Functions and mechanisms in structural-modelling explanations
Abstract: One way the social scientists explain phenomena is by building structural models. These models are explanatory insofar as they manage to perform a recursive decomposition on an initial multivariate probability distribution, which can be interpreted as a mechanism. The social scientists should include the variables in the model on the basis of their function in the mechanism. This paper examines the notion of ‘function’ within structural modelling. We argue that ‘functions’ ought to be understood as the theoretical underpinnings of the causes, namely as the role that causes play in the functioning of the mechanism.
Keywords: causality, explanation, function, mechanism, recursive decomposition, structural modelling
