Author-Name: MELITZ, Jacques
Author-Workplace-Name: ENSAE and CEPII, Paris, and CEPR, London
Author-Name: TOUBAL, Farid
Author-Workplace-Name: Ecole Normale Supérieure de Cachan, France
Title: Foreign language learnings: An econometric analysis
Abstract: The paper is devoted to an econometric analysis of learning foreign languages in all parts of the world. Our sample covers 193 countries and 13 important languages. Four factors significantly explain learning. All four affect the broad decision to learn but the last two also point to the choice of the particular language to learn. Literacy promotes learning in general while the world population of speakers of the native language discourages it. Trade with speakers of a specific language prompts learning of that specific language while the linguistic distance between the home and the foreign language discourages learning of that specific language. Trade may well deserve more emphasis than the other three factors (literacy rate, linguistic distance, and world population of native speakers), not only for its high significance, but also because its direction can change faster and by a larger order of magnitude. Controlling for individual acquired languages, including English, is of no particular importance.
Classification-JEL: F10, F20, Z00, J00
Keywords: language learning, language and trade, English as a global language
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2014049
Creation-Date: 2014-11-18
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2014.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2014049

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: DECANCQ, Koen
Author-Email: koen.decancq@uantwerp.be
Author-Workplace-Name: University Antwerp
Author-Name: NEUMANN, Dirk
Author-Email: dirk.neumann@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, CORE, Belgium
Title: Does the choice of well-being measure matter empirically? An illustration with German data
Abstract: We discuss and compare five measures of individual well-being, namely income, an objective composite well-being index, a measure of subjective well-being, equivalent income, and a well-being measure based on the von Neumann-Morgenstern utilities of the individuals. After examining the information requirements of these measures, we illustrate their implementation using data from the German Socio-Economic Panel (SOEP) for 2010. We find sizeable differences in the characteristics of the individuals identified as worst off according to the different well-being measures. Less than 1% of the individuals belong to the bottom decile according to all five measures. Moreover, the measures lead to considerably different well-being rankings of the individuals. These findings highlight the importance of the choice of well-being measure for policy making. 
Classification-JEL: D31, D63, I30
Keywords: income, composite well-being index, life satisfaction, equivalent income, von Neumann-Morgenstern utility function, worst off, Germany
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, CORE
Number: 2014050
Creation-Date: 2014-10-31
File-URL: https://sites.uclouvain.be/core/publications/coredp/coredp2014.html
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:cor:louvco:2014050

